{"title":"*Fear of Data*","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/969780677/0/marginalrevolution~Fear-of-Data.html","date":1790238245000,"content":"<p>The author is Omri Ben-Shahar, and the subtitle is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Fear-Data-Privacy-Regulation-Astray_and/dp/0674297865/ref=sr_1_1?crid=B5HHB3F7Y8I5&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.78uNZS_3eBwDup6cnldbOJ4DNcCOzWmuWKkaJWeEGUi7JgMuFpgG26B0dk7NOHATcnt7GMaJ9QF0DpUsk98d7H8IJPSMy8pr27wlR5M8pg3CLs_-G7Bz1aobtxg_HTeizJBCqJC7G0GWrjPaxoP7dw.Jj3PZMGvW1p3XOPhM-lj0QU7lSHAP9wKfk4fHtDAZTo&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Omri+Ben-Shahar&amp;qid=1790086971&amp;sprefix=%2Caps%2C215&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">How Privacy Panic Led Tech Regulation Astray — and How to Fix It</a>.  I would describe this book as bracing, and full of substantive engagement.  Basically the author wishes to give privacy considerations less weight in social decisions.  Excerpt:</p>\r\n<blockquote><p>What is the concrete evidence for the benefits of facial recognition technology in investigation of  human-trafficking crimes?  I would love to have found global estimates of the magnitude — of the trafficking victims rescued through the most advanced facial recognition methods — but all I have is a collage of reports [reports are then described].</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>One chapter is entitled “The Futility of Personal Rights.”  Agree or not, this book is full of actual arguments, so I approve.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/09/fear-of-data.html\">*Fear of Data*</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/969780677/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/969780677/marginalrevolution\"><img 